// Right recursion cannot reduce anything until it has read the whole input, so the parse stack grows by one state per
// item and only unwinds at the end. The trace is every shift first and every reduction afterwards, which is the exact
// opposite of [left-recursion] on the same input, and it is the case which holds a driver to a parse stack which really
// grows.
//
// Forty items is not deep enough to force the Go driver to grow its stack, which starts out with room for 1024, and it
// is not meant to be: a trace long enough for that is no longer a trace anyone reviews, and grammars of that scale
// belong to the scale tier which runs the real grammars. Forty is past any small fixed size a hand written runner might
// start out with, while keeping the trace short enough to read in one screen.
//
// The scanner is not what this case is about, so its only rule is a single character.

@scanner {
    WHITESPACE: /[ \t\n]+/ @skip;

    ITEM: "i";
}

@parser {
    list
        : ITEM list
        | ITEM
        ;
}
